in the brave new world of steam early  access there's only one host daring  intrepid and resourceful enough to steer  you past the pitfalls of paying for  games that are still in development and  until we find that person I'm your host  Chris waters in today's episode we've  got dispatches from our far flung  correspondents around the globe about  games that will broaden your horizons  first up is Starbound Maxwell McGee  spent a lot of time delving deep  underground to bring you this report  because either he doesn't know where  stars are or I've grossly misunderstood  the very foundation of this game  starbound is a game of exploration  item-crafting monster slaying and  occasional intergalactic piracy in fact  it's far too expansive to pigeonhole  into one single category in the  beginning a short series of introductory  missions guide you through the basics  but the training wheels soon fall off  and you're left to engage the wild as  you deem fit will you construct great  buildings or forge mighty weapons maybe  you'll dig a really deep hole and die in  it actually you'll almost certainly end  up dying in some deep dark pit such as  the cold uncaring nature of Starbound  however the harshness of this world  while cruel at times also makes your  victories feel that much sweeter and as  you progress further in the game and  your understanding of how its many  different parts and systems all fit  together it's a delight to see the list  of things you can do as a player grow  and expand in unexpected ways this is  especially true when you save up enough  fuel to travel to a distant planet and  Rob the locals blind hey such as the  cold uncaring nature of Starbound when I  asked Danny O'Dwyer what the game  secrets of rate akan was he told me it  was about birds flying about and birds  hopefully he's flesh those ideas out of  it or else this next segment is going to  be really short  secrets of rado con is a game from  broken rules the Austrian masterminds  behind one of my favorite steam releases  of all time and yet it moves and much  like the papercraft platformer it's not  an easy game to sum up essentially this  is the spiritual successor to their  second game chasing aurora which came  out on the wii u in 2012 in it you play  as a solitary bird swooping through the  landscapes diving into caves and soaring  into the air through wind currents this  is a game about unraveling the secrets  of the landscape you solve puzzles  collect trinkets from civilizations past  and interact with the various fauna that  inhabits this wonderfully angular world  you can defend yourself with plants or  sacrifice smaller birds to your prey to  distract them or just chuck bunnies  around in terms of early access it  appears to be on the home stretch the  studio has been working on it for about  three years and the version I played is  stable and looks and sounds fantastic  you get access to the entire main camp a  and except for the grand finale the  reveal of the secret itself it also  comes at a pretty full-on editor which  you can create your own levels that will  be shareable via Steam Workshop in true  broken rules form this game is available  on PC Mac and Linux the nine dollars 99  cents with twenty five percent off this  first week this is about as close to a  full release as you're likely to get on  steam early access so if you're in the  mood to play something clever and  incredibly unique pick up secrets of red  oak on today our dreams can take us too  fantastical places frighten us with  lurking horrors or take us back in time  to high school where we're late for  class and don't know our schedule and  forgot the code or a locker and have no  shoes Jess McDonald drifted into dream  for this next segment where fortunately  things turned out to be a bit more  interesting than your average recurring  anxiety dream  from the outset dream feels a bit like  gone home a daunting house dotted with  dark hallways serves as the foundation  for the game world but is in no way the  extent of it dream is a first-person  exploration game which takes place  outside of reality for the most part or  at least outside of the corporeal waking  world you traverse places created  through the lucid dreaming of your  protagonist Howard Phillips which extend  from great white rooms punctuated by  menacing black cubes to sprawling  deserts it all means something as dreams  often do you just need to find out what  so far the gameplay appears to hop  between puzzling exploration and long  narrative segments which illustrate some  of the plot there are moments a very  effective horror tangled into the lucid  dreaming experience which include a  puzzle of escaping a pursuing shadow  monster in a maze while trying to  activate an elevator or seeming as if  you've awoken only to read a message  scrawled in blood on your bathroom wall  the currently in alpha game isn't  without its bugs or occasional T diems  where it's unclear what you're supposed  to achieve particularly when sequences  can repeat themselves that aside the  overall intrigue of the story and the  beautifully detailed game world keep  dream feeling like it's brimming with  potential on top of that the oculus rift  support at launch really does just cinch  the deal and with that I'll leave you to  go off on virtual adventures of your own  but before you go let us know in the  comments below which of the games so far  has piqued your interest the most thanks  for watching and we'll see you tomorrow
 

